{"agent_id":"pp3g-aWah-open-ended-architectural-review-on-issue-367-read-the","agent_seq":1,"timestamp":"2026-08-14T23:46:00.950803356Z","event":{"type":"CommentAdded","issue_uuid":"9cb2ccd9-0603-4075-93f8-2293e63181c9","comment_uuid":"4cb253c0-7f6e-47d6-9ac8-f5395b0b14d3","author":"pp3g-aWah-open-ended-architectural-review-on-issue-367-read-the","content":"Plan: Independent architectural judgment on the Tools/monorepo drift architecture. Approach: (1) I have thoroughly explored the codebase — the crosslink fork's embedding mechanism (build.rs + init/mod.rs), the Tools repo structure, the deployed copies in ASES, the live wrappers in ~/.local/bin, the model plugins in ~/.config/opencode, and the hook-config.json precedence bug. (2) Core finding: the crosslink fork ALREADY has a working atomic distribution mechanism (binary embedding + crosslink init) for hooks/skills/commands/rules/mcp, but guard plugins, model plugins, CLI wrappers, and agent definitions are NOT embedded — they're distributed via manual copy from Tools, which is why they drift. (3) My verdict will argue that the right architecture is to EXTEND the binary embedding mechanism to cover ALL tooling resources, making drift structurally impossible rather than detectable after the fact. This eliminates the need for sync-tooling.sh, sha256 manifests, state machines, and --promote flags. (4) I will distinguish must-change from optional, include WHAT-NOT-TESTED, and be concrete about what to embed and how. Read-only: no file changes, no commits.","created_at":"2026-08-14T23:46:00.940857910Z","kind":"plan","signed_by":"SHA256:pfTzP29ngEGfQm87RqGLOnykBkYfo8upB1axgAWtxTU","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgvF2YsnQTnF7UcAEpO9wPzTpHDDEeESUCEAZ1xaKs3woAAAARY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWNvbW1lbnQAAAAAAAAABnNoYTUxMgAAAFMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAABAnjAhpDTIllkx/JEaPv975zfNtrEgTYJH/Hl7/9ax2xzceOBN/yalJGgCXoomWOz+hGl5gNJovsGK0BIG4EskBA=="},"signed_by":"SHA256:pfTzP29ngEGfQm87RqGLOnykBkYfo8upB1axgAWtxTU","signature":